{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\": \"R-6:73\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The repeated definite al-ḥaqq lets the mutual counsel of 103:3 resonate with 6:73, where creation is by truth and God's own saying is truth; the ethical charge sits within a divinely true order.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:73\", \"ar\": \"خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:73\", \"ar\": \"قَوْلُهُ ٱلْحَقُّ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus makes al-ḥaqq the first payload of the community's mutual charge.\", \"limits\": \"6:73 does not depict mutual counsel or specify the social content of al-ḥaqq.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-6:85\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The focus names al-ṣāliḥāt as deeds, while 6:85 places prophets among al-ṣāliḥīn; the shared root links the deed class with a prophetic category without making the forms equivalent.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:85\", \"ar\": \"كُلٌّۭ مِّنَ ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exception identifies its members through doing deeds from the ṣ-l-ḥ word family.\", \"limits\": \"6:85 does not specify these deeds or connect the prophets to mutual counsel.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-6:114\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The community's al-ḥaqq can include recognizing revelation as true: 6:114 says the Book is sent from the Lord in truth and warns against doubt.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:114\", \"ar\": \"أَنَّهُۥ مُنَزَّلٌۭ مِّن رَّبِّكَ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:114\", \"ar\": \"فَلَا تَكُونَنَّ مِنَ ٱلْمُمْتَرِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus leaves al-ḥaqq without an explicit object, allowing revelation to be one possible content of the counsel.\", \"limits\": \"6:114 addresses the Messenger and Book recipients; it does not say that they are carrying out mutual counsel.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-6:144\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"A different form of the same w-ṣ-y root appears in the challenge over whether God prescribed the claimed dietary rule. Beside that charge of fabrication, the focus's reciprocal tawāṣī bi-l-ḥaqq can sound like counsel answerable to truth rather than false attribution.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:144\", \"ar\": \"أَمْ كُنتُمْ شُهَدَآءَ إِذْ وَصَّىٰكُمُ ٱللَّهُ بِهَٰذَا\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:144\", \"ar\": \"فَمَنْ أَظْلَمُ مِمَّنِ ٱفْتَرَىٰ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ كَذِبًۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus repeats tawāṣaw for truth and patience; 6:144 puts a divine prescription claim under scrutiny.\", \"limits\": \"The focus uses reciprocal Form VI counsel, while 6:144 uses a divine prescription form; the verses do not refer to the same ruling.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-6:151\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus's al-ḥaqq gains a concrete ethical register in 6:151: God's charge includes a limit on taking life, which is permitted only bi-l-ḥaqq. The shared w-ṣ-y root also places mutual counsel beside divine prescription.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:151\", \"ar\": \"وَلَا تَقْتُلُوا۟ ٱلنَّفْسَ ٱلَّتِى حَرَّمَ ٱللَّهُ إِلَّا بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:151\", \"ar\": \"ذَٰلِكُمْ وَصَّىٰكُم بِهِۦ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus combines tawāṣaw with al-ḥaqq, and 6:151 combines a life-protecting rule with God's wasiyya.\", \"limits\": \"Divine prescription in 6:151 is not itself reciprocal counsel, and the passage does not identify its addressees with the focus group.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-6:152\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus's al-ḥaqq can be heard as fair dealings and just speech: 6:152 commands equitable measure and justice when speaking, then calls these rules God's charge.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:152\", \"ar\": \"وَأَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ وَٱلْمِيزَانَ بِٱلْقِسْطِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:152\", \"ar\": \"وَإِذَا قُلْتُمْ فَٱعْدِلُوا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"6:152\", \"ar\": \"ذَٰلِكُمْ وَصَّىٰكُم بِهِۦ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both the focus's truth charge and 6:152's divine charge concern what a community transmits and practices.\", \"limits\": \"6:152 does not use al-ḥaqq for these particular rules or describe mutual exhortation.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:8\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Al-ḥaqq is the weighing on Judgment Day, and the heavy scales mark success while the next verse names the light-scaled as losers. Beside 103:2–3, the truth-and-patience group can be heard against a final measure that resolves the surah's loss verdict.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:2\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ ٱلْإِنسَٰنَ لَفِى خُسْرٍ\"}, {\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:8\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلْوَزْنُ يَوْمَئِذٍ ٱلْحَقُّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:9\", \"ar\": \"وَمَنْ خَفَّتْ مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ خَسِرُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُم\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus opens its exception from a verdict of loss and includes al-ḥaqq among the group's defining practices.\", \"limits\": \"7:8–9 does not say which specific deeds are weighed or identify the weighed group with those of 103:3.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:35\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The conditional group who guards against wrong and sets things right is promised freedom from fear and grief, a related escape pattern to the focus's exception from loss.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:35\", \"ar\": \"فَمَنِ ٱتَّقَىٰ وَأَصْلَحَ فَلَا خَوْفٌ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا هُمْ يَحْزَنُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages distinguish a qualified group after addressing a wider human audience.\", \"limits\": \"7:35 does not mention mutual counsel, patience, or the focus's exact faith-and-deeds formula.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:42\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"7:42 repeats the focus's exact faith-and-righteous-deeds formula, promises Paradise, and adds that no soul is burdened beyond its capacity. The focus extends this shared profile with mutual truth and patience.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:42\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:42\", \"ar\": \"لَا نُكَلِّفُ نَفْسًا إِلَّا وُسْعَهَآ\"}], \"activation\": \"The identical opening predicates make this a direct parallel to the first half of the focus's exception.\", \"limits\": \"7:42 does not mention reciprocal exhortation or say that its group has the focus's full set of traits.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:56\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The focus's al-ṣāliḥāt can take on a restorative, public sense beside 7:56, which forbids corruption after the earth has been set right and associates mercy with the muḥsinūn.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:56\", \"ar\": \"وَلَا تُفْسِدُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ بَعْدَ إِصْلَٰحِهَا\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:56\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ رَحْمَتَ ٱللَّهِ قَرِيبٌۭ مِّنَ ٱلْمُحْسِنِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared ṣ-l-ḥ root and explicit contrast with corruption make social repair a possible register for righteous deeds.\", \"limits\": \"7:56 does not define al-ṣāliḥāt or say that the focus group performs this particular command.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:85\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"Shuʿayb's call gives public justice a concrete form: fulfill measures, do not diminish people's due, and do not corrupt the land after its repair. This makes economic rights one possible field of the focus's al-ḥaqq and al-ṣāliḥāt.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:85\", \"ar\": \"فَأَوْفُوا۟ ٱلْكَيْلَ وَٱلْمِيزَانَ وَلَا تَبْخَسُوا۟ ٱلنَّاسَ أَشْيَآءَهُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:85\", \"ar\": \"وَلَا تُفْسِدُوا۟ فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ بَعْدَ إِصْلَٰحِهَا\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus names righteous deeds and truth; Shuʿayb's passage supplies examples of just communal conduct and repair.\", \"limits\": \"7:85 does not use al-ḥaqq or tawāṣaw, and this is one prophetic community's application rather than a definition of the focus.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:126\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The magicians publicly affirm faith in God's signs and ask God to pour patience upon them before their deaths. Their scene presents patience as a needed resource under coercion, while the focus makes it a mutual communal charge.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:126\", \"ar\": \"ءَامَنَّا بِـَٔايَٰتِ رَبِّنَا\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:126\", \"ar\": \"رَبَّنَآ أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنَا صَبْرًۭا وَتَوَفَّنَا مُسْلِمِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus pairs faith with patience; 7:126 joins faith and ṣabr in a collective confession under threat.\", \"limits\": \"The magicians ask God for patience rather than exhorting one another, and the scenes are not identified as the same event.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:142\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Moses's handoff to Aaron makes reform among the people a charge for the period of the leader's absence, countered by the path of corrupters; it is a leadership-shaped parallel to the focus's communal practice.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:142\", \"ar\": \"ٱخْلُفْنِى فِى قَوْمِى وَأَصْلِحْ وَلَا تَتَّبِعْ سَبِيلَ ٱلْمُفْسِدِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus pairs righteous action with mutual charge; Moses explicitly entrusts his people to Aaron with a reform command.\", \"limits\": \"7:142 is a one-way instruction to a named successor, not reciprocal counsel among a group.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:168\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"Within the dispersed communities of 7:168, some are righteous and some are not; this echoes the focus's boundary-making exception, where a moral class is distinguished from humanity at large.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:168\", \"ar\": \"مِّنْهُمُ ٱلصَّٰلِحُونَ وَمِنْهُمْ دُونَ ذَٰلِكَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages mark righteousness as a subset rather than treating a whole population as uniform.\", \"limits\": \"7:168 does not specify the deeds that make some righteous or mention truth and patience.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:170\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In 7:169–170, a generation is condemned for taking worldly gain and violating its covenant about speaking truth of God; the following group holds fast to the Book and is rewarded as reformers. This connects the focus's al-ḥaqq with sustained righteous practice rather than claims alone.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:169\", \"ar\": \"أَن لَّا يَقُولُوا۟ عَلَى ٱللَّهِ إِلَّا ٱلْحَقَّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:170\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلَّذِينَ يُمَسِّكُونَ بِٱلْكِتَٰبِ وَأَقَامُوا۟ ٱلصَّلَوٰةَ إِنَّا لَا نُضِيعُ أَجْرَ ٱلْمُصْلِحِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus joins truth to deeds; this passage sets truthful speech, scriptural fidelity, prayer, and reform in sequence.\", \"limits\": \"7:170 does not name mutual exhortation or patience, and its immediate actors are a specific scriptural community.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:190\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The same ṣ-l-ḥ root appears in 7:189–190 as the indefinite ṣāliḥan hoped for and then received, followed by the partners they associate. This is a root echo, but it is not the focus's plural label for righteous deeds.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:189\", \"ar\": \"لَئِنْ ءَاتَيْتَنَا صَٰلِحًۭا لَّنَكُونَنَّ مِنَ ٱلشَّٰكِرِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:190\", \"ar\": \"فَلَمَّآ ءَاتَىٰهُمَا صَٰلِحًۭا جَعَلَا لَهُۥ شُرَكَآءَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The root match invites comparison, and the subsequent shirk makes it a boundary on reading the root as a guarantee of faithful conduct.\", \"limits\": \"The supplied verses do not settle what ṣāliḥan refers to, and it does not denote a plural class of deeds here.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:196\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"At the surah's close, Allah is said to take charge of the righteous. This adds divine protection as a possible outcome for the righteous class named in the focus.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:196\", \"ar\": \"وَهُوَ يَتَوَلَّى ٱلصَّٰلِحِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages mark a group through the ṣ-l-ḥ root, and 7:196 explicitly assigns Allah as their protector.\", \"limits\": \"7:196 does not state that those it protects are the same people as the focus group or mention mutual exhortation.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-7:199\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The command to enjoin al-maʿrūf gives recognized good a public voice, a nearby ethical function for the focus's al-ḥaqq; here it is a Messenger's directive rather than mutual counsel.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"7:199\", \"ar\": \"خُذِ ٱلْعَفْوَ وَأْمُرْ بِٱلْعُرْفِ وَأَعْرِضْ عَنِ ٱلْجَٰهِلِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages connect right conduct with exhorting others.\", \"limits\": \"Al-maʿrūf and al-ḥaqq are not identical terms, and 7:199 addresses the Messenger singularly.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:1\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The believers in 8:1 are told to repair what lies between them; its iṣlāḥ shares the ṣ-l-ḥ root with the focus's al-ṣāliḥāt and gives righteous action a specifically relational form.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:1\", \"ar\": \"فَٱتَّقُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ وَأَصْلِحُوا۟ ذَاتَ بَيْنِكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:1\", \"ar\": \"إِن كُنتُم مُّؤْمِنِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus defines an excepted group by faith and righteous deeds; 8:1 addresses believers and commands repair between them.\", \"limits\": \"8:1 does not mention mutual exhortation to truth or patience.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:8\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In the battle setting, God's purpose is to establish al-ḥaqq and invalidate falsehood. This gives the focus's mutual counsel toward al-ḥaqq a public, contested dimension: truth is established against what opposes it.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:7\", \"ar\": \"وَيُرِيدُ ٱللَّهُ أَن يُحِقَّ ٱلْحَقَّ بِكَلِمَٰتِهِۦ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:8\", \"ar\": \"لِيُحِقَّ ٱلْحَقَّ وَيُبْطِلَ ٱلْبَٰطِلَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact al-ḥaqq of the focus recurs in a scene where truth's establishment is God's stated purpose.\", \"limits\": \"8:7–8 does not describe reciprocal advice, and the battle outcome is not identified with the focus group's practice.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:24\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"The plural address to believers makes response to God's life-giving call a collective duty; beside the focus, it frames faith as an active response that can be sustained through communal truth and patience.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:24\", \"ar\": \"يَٰٓأَيُّهَا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ ٱسْتَجِيبُوا۟ لِلَّهِ وَلِلرَّسُولِ إِذَا دَعَاكُمْ لِمَا يُحْيِيكُمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages address believers in the plural and attach an active practice to faith.\", \"limits\": \"8:24 does not mention counsel, al-ḥaqq, or al-ṣabr.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"medium\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:46\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In a battle setting, believers are told not to dispute, to stand firm, and to be patient; this gives the focus's mutual counsel in patience a concrete role in preserving communal strength under pressure.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:45\", \"ar\": \"إِذَا لَقِيتُمْ فِئَةًۭ فَٱثْبُتُوا۟ وَٱذْكُرُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ كَثِيرًۭا لَّعَلَّكُمْ تُفْلِحُونَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:46\", \"ar\": \"وَلَا تَنَٰزَعُوا۟ فَتَفْشَلُوا۟ وَتَذْهَبَ رِيحُكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:46\", \"ar\": \"وَٱصْبِرُوٓا۟ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ مَعَ ٱلصَّٰبِرِينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus makes patience reciprocal; 8:46 directly connects patience with avoiding conflict among believers.\", \"limits\": \"8:46 commands the group rather than describing members counseling one another, and its immediate setting is combat.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:63\", \"status\": \"open\", \"finding\": \"The focus's reciprocal practice could be heard within a community whose hearts are joined by God, not by human expenditure; this would make mutual counsel a practice inside divinely given solidarity.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:63\", \"ar\": \"وَلَٰكِنَّ ٱللَّهَ أَلَّفَ بَيْنَهُمْ\"}], \"missing\": \"The verses do not identify their communities or explicitly connect mutual exhortation with the joining of hearts; another passage linking counsel to God-given unity could establish that relation.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-8:74\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The believers of 8:74 enact communal support by giving refuge and aid, and are called true believers. This offers a practical parallel to the focus's mutual sustaining through truth and patience, without reducing counsel to material aid.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"8:74\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلَّذِينَ ءَاوَوا۟ وَّنَصَرُوٓا۟ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ حَقًّۭا\"}], \"activation\": \"Both passages make believers a plural group whose defining life includes action toward one another.\", \"limits\": \"8:74 describes refuge and aid, not mutual verbal counsel, and its context is migration and struggle.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-9:71\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The believing women and men are allies to one another and command right while forbidding wrong; this gives a close communal example of the focus's reciprocal charge, with an explicitly mixed-gender community.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"9:71\", \"ar\": \"وَٱلْمُؤْمِنُونَ وَٱلْمُؤْمِنَٰتُ بَعْضُهُمْ أَوْلِيَآءُ بَعْضٍۢ\"}, {\"ref\": \"9:71\", \"ar\": \"يَأْمُرُونَ بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ وَيَنْهَوْنَ عَنِ ٱلْمُنكَرِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus's reciprocal tawāṣī is closely paralleled by mutual alliance and plural commanding and forbidding.\", \"limits\": \"9:71 names al-maʿrūf and al-munkar rather than al-ḥaqq and al-ṣabr.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-9:102\", \"status\": \"note\", \"finding\": \"9:102 describes people who mix a righteous deed with another bad one and remain subject to hoped-for repentance. It cautions against reading al-ṣāliḥāt as a claim that the group's record is flawless.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"9:102\", \"ar\": \"خَلَطُوا۟ عَمَلًۭا صَٰلِحًۭا وَءَاخَرَ سَيِّئًا عَسَى ٱللَّهُ أَن يَتُوبَ عَلَيْهِمْ\"}], \"activation\": \"The shared ṣ-l-ḥ vocabulary is set beside an explicit mixture of good and bad action.\", \"limits\": \"9:102 does not identify these people with the focus's excepted group or mention mutual truth and patience.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-9:112\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"9:112 portrays a community through active practices, including enjoining what is recognized as right, forbidding wrong, and keeping God's limits. This is a fuller social profile parallel to the focus's compressed mutual charge.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"9:112\", \"ar\": \"ٱلْءَامِرُونَ بِٱلْمَعْرُوفِ وَٱلنَّاهُونَ عَنِ ٱلْمُنكَرِ وَٱلْحَٰفِظُونَ لِحُدُودِ ٱللَّهِ\"}], \"activation\": \"Both describe a plural moral community through what its members practice and transmit.\", \"limits\": \"9:112 does not include patience or use the focus's reciprocal verb.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:4\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"10:4 repeats the focus's faith-and-righteous-deeds formula and places its reward under al-qisṭ at the return to God. The focus supplies a communal practice profile within this just final accounting.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:4\", \"ar\": \"لِيَجْزِىَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ بِٱلْقِسْطِ\"}], \"activation\": \"The exact shared formula links the focus's qualified people to a passage about return and recompense.\", \"limits\": \"10:4 does not list the focus's mutual practices or equate al-qisṭ with al-ḥaqq.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:9\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"After repeating the focus's exact faith-and-righteous-deeds formula, 10:9 says their Lord guides them by their faith. The focus adds mutual truth and patience as communal practices alongside that guiding faith.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:9\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ يَهْدِيهِمْ رَبُّهُم بِإِيمَٰنِهِم\"}], \"activation\": \"The repeated predicate pair makes 10:9 a direct comparison for the focus's opening conditions.\", \"limits\": \"10:9 does not state that mutual exhortation is a condition of guidance or name the focus's additional practices.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:32\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"10:32 names Allah as al-ḥaqq and says that what lies beyond truth is error. This anchors the focus's definite al-ḥaqq in a divine and sharp truth–error boundary, not merely in agreeable advice.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:32\", \"ar\": \"فَذَٰلِكُمُ ٱللَّهُ رَبُّكُمُ ٱلْحَقُّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:32\", \"ar\": \"فَمَاذَا بَعْدَ ٱلْحَقِّ إِلَّا ٱلضَّلَٰلُ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus leaves al-ḥaqq definite and does not specify its referent.\", \"limits\": \"10:32 does not define the particular subjects believers should counsel one another about.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:53\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In 10:53 al-ḥaqq is the affirmed reality of the threatened judgment, and 10:54 says judgment between wrongdoers is by al-qisṭ. This gives the focus's counsel toward truth an eschatological dimension with consequences that cannot be evaded.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:53\", \"ar\": \"قُلْ إِى وَرَبِّىٓ إِنَّهُۥ لَحَقٌّۭ ۖ وَمَآ أَنتُم بِمُعْجِزِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:54\", \"ar\": \"وَقُضِىَ بَيْنَهُم بِٱلْقِسْطِ ۚ وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُونَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus's definite al-ḥaqq is paired here with an oath affirming a judgment and with fair adjudication.\", \"limits\": \"10:53–54 do not directly identify mutual counsel as a response to this warning.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:81\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"In Moses's contest, God invalidates the magicians' work and does not set right the work of corrupters; the next verse says God establishes al-ḥaqq. This brings the focus's righteous deeds and truth into a scene where false display is exposed.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:81\", \"ar\": \"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ لَا يُصْلِحُ عَمَلَ ٱلْمُفْسِدِينَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:82\", \"ar\": \"وَيُحِقُّ ٱللَّهُ ٱلْحَقَّ بِكَلِمَٰتِهِۦ\"}], \"activation\": \"The focus joins ṣāliḥ deeds with al-ḥaqq; this passage sets reform, corrupters, and truth in contrast.\", \"limits\": \"The focus does not mention magic or claim that every act of counsel occurs in a public contest.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"R-10:109\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"After announcing the truth from God to all people, 10:109 tells the Messenger alone to follow revelation and be patient until God judges. The focus turns this prophetic endurance into mutual counsel among the believing community.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلْحَقِّ وَتَوَاصَوْا۟ بِٱلصَّبْرِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:108\", \"ar\": \"قَدْ جَآءَكُمُ ٱلْحَقُّ مِن رَّبِّكُمْ\"}, {\"ref\": \"10:109\", \"ar\": \"وَٱتَّبِعْ مَا يُوحَىٰٓ إِلَيْكَ وَٱصْبِرْ حَتَّىٰ يَحْكُمَ ٱللَّهُ\"}], \"activation\": \"The adjacent verses join al-ḥaqq with patience and waiting for God's judgment, as the focus joins truth with patience.\", \"limits\": \"10:109 addresses the Messenger in the singular; it does not itself command a community to counsel one another.\", \"support\": \"strong\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}\n{\"id\": \"X1\", \"status\": \"reading\", \"finding\": \"The Fatiha's daily plural plea to be guided along the straight path, followed by its distinction between those favored and those who stray, makes the focus's exception legible as a shared path with recognizable companions and boundaries.\", \"evidence\": [{\"ref\": \"103:3\", \"ar\": \"إِلَّا ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوا۟ وَعَمِلُوا۟ ٱلصَّٰلِحَٰتِ\"}, {\"ref\": \"1:6\", \"ar\": \"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}, {\"ref\": \"1:7\", \"ar\": \"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}], \"activation\": \"The supplied context notes that the Fatiha is recited in every salah, and both passages distinguish a guided group from contrary paths.\", \"limits\": \"The Fatiha does not name al-ḥaqq or al-ṣabr, and the favored group is not explicitly equated with the focus's exception.\", \"support\": \"medium\", \"relevance\": \"high\"}"}}
